            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 23-year-old male patient, admitted on 17/02/2015 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. Physical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Diagnostic workup including blood tests and abdominal ultrasound confirmed the diagnosis. The patient was commenced on intravenous fluids and analgesics. Given the acute presentation, a laparoscopic emergency appendectomy was performed on 18/02/2015 without intraoperative complications. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Wound care was provided, vital signs remained stable throughout, and the patient was mobilised early. He was discharged home on 21/02/2015 in good general condition with oral analgesia and wound care instructions. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks. Do not hesitate to contact me should you have any questions.</div>"
            },
